s grown  quite quickly in the last while so since  2003 we've asked a similar life  satisfaction question on the general  social survey or what I'll refer to as  the GSS and since 2009 we've been asking  the same question on the Canadian  community health survey which I'll refer  to as the cchs the GSS is an annual  survey of approximately fifteen to  twenty five thousand respondents and  each year the GSS psycho collects  information on a thematic topic such as  time use victimization family in 2013 it  was on social belonging and social  identity and the cchs is an annual  survey of 65,000 respondents that  collects information on various health  issues on both of those surveys for the  years i've shown up there we ask this  question using a scale of 0 to 10 where  0 means very dissatisfied and 10 means  very satisfied how do you feel about  your life as a whole  right now and this is the evaluative  component we're not asking about people  how happy they feel at that point in  time but we're asking them to evaluate  it in a more abstract sense if you wish  now the availability of these this  question across this wide range of  surveys allows us to do a couple of  things the first thing that allowed us  to do was to compare the life  satisfaction question across a broad  range of survey context to see how  people respond to the question was the  question volatile the people seem to get  it does it depend a lot on what the  context and on what we're asking them  and are the cove area to the factors  that are related to life satisfaction  consistent across surveys and over time  and so we did some work on this and what  we discovered was survey respondents are  able and willing to answer the life  satisfaction question very few people  refuse or say they don't know or leave  it blank there was a few small changes  on the life satisfaction question since  2003 but those had a minimal impact on  overall response patterns when we use  the GSS time use survey and we knew  exactly what day and one month and so on  that people completed this survey we  found that the life satisfaction  responses are not at all correlated with  the day of the week that people  completed the survey or the month of the  year in which they completed the survey  and we see very high level of  consistency in responses across the cchs  that is the canadian community health  serving with its consistent survey  context but greater variability across  the general social survey and we see  that this is a this is correlated with  the other types of content that's in  there and importantly we also see that  the direction and the magnitude of the  relationships between socioeconomic  characteristics like being unemployed or  having a higher or lower income or age  or education or marital status are very  consistent across these surveys so the  long and the short of this is it games  gives us a great deal of confidence in  what we're measuring has validity and is  meaningful to people and this is very  consistent with the broad literature in  the academic world as  well the other thing that having all of  these surveys and these questions on  these various surveys and survey years  is it allows us to pool the data into  into group it and what this does is it  provides very large sample sizes that  any single survey would not allow to  address questions that any single survey  would not allow us to address so for  this study that I'm going to turn to in  a moment we used the three or four  cycles or so of the of the cchs and I  think five cycles with GSS and it gave  us a pooled sample of almost three  hundred and forty thousand Canadians  over the period from 2009 to 2013 and as  the side will be able to add another  130,000 people to that sample in a few  months from now so we've got a  tremendous depth here by which we can  look at Canadian society and this is  very this is very useful so what are the  responses of these individuals look like  simple distribution here what we see is  that average life satisfaction in Canada  is just a hair under eight point Oh 8.0  out of the scale of 10 and there are  there no thresholds above or below which  someone is deemed to be satisfied or  dissatisfied with their life but what we  see here is that thirty-eight percent of  Canadians score and nine or ten on the  scale and seventy percent score an eight  nine or ten so generally people are  quite satisfied in Canada and that's  certainly consistent with the world  happiness report where Canada ranks  quite high on an international standing  if we look at the lower end of the scale  about fourteen percent of Canadians are  about one in seven have a life  satisfaction score of six or less now  with that backdrop in mind let's take a  look across cities and what we're using  here is the statscan concept of the  census metropolitan area which is the  urban area of Vancouver or wherever so  what we see here is average life  satisfaction and get just coming back  not to get too hung up on sample size  here but in Vancouver we have 17,000  respond  London's who completed this question  over that time period in Toronto we had  30,000 respondents so again very robust  estimates here so in the midpoint we see  Canada with an average life satisfaction  of just under eight point 0 and the  green bars above are the census  metropolitan areas that have life  satisfaction that's significantly higher  compared to the Canadian average and  you'll notice these these whisker plots  here this is the ninety-five percent  confidence interval so there's there's  error around these estimates so in  Calgary for example the estimate could  be this is the real estimate here or  what we observe it could be as low as  this or it could be as high as that so  it gives you some sense of what's the  magnitude of error and for some very  small CMA such as abbotsford you see  that this confidence interval is a  little bit wider so if we're going to  compare say Kelowna and Montreal while  you see there's an overlapping there so  don't make too much of this small  difference so what do we see saggin a  toile riviere st. John's Greater Sudbury  Quebec many sea mas consistently quite  higher up at around eight point two on  our ten point scale down towards the  bottom the orange bars are those that  are statistically significant from the  canadian average so toronto vancouver  windsor with average life satisfaction  scores around 7.8 now what do we want to  make about this degree of magnitude I  think the first important point to be  made here is when we compare life  satisfaction across countries of the  world the range of difference is about  10 times bigger than what we see across  these 33 census metropolitan areas so  this is a very constrained distribution  relative to the international context  but nonetheless we have of almost a  point 45 spread between the lowest and  the highest CMA on life satisfaction and  when we look at marital status or  unemployment status that's a similar  coefficient to what we're seeing across  across that and I'll come back to that  in a moment now averages tell us one  thing but let's look at some  specific spots along the distribution  this chart shows the percent of  individuals rating their life  satisfaction as a 9 or a 10 I've used  the nine and the 10 in quite an  arbitrary way and what we see here is  you know again at the top we've got  upwards of forty to forty-five percent  of Canadians in these specific sea mas  with a nine or ten on their life  satisfaction down to less than thirty  five percent in Toronto Vancouver Barry  Edmonton so overall approximately a  11-point percentage point difference in  the share of Canadians across sea mas  who are at the high end if we broaden  this and say okay well what about those  guys who scored eight they're pretty  happy too well if we look at that in who  scored eight nine or ten and we have a  14 percentage point difference across  census metropolitan areas at the other  end of the scale we saw earlier about  fourteen percent of people score six or  less on the life satisfaction scale and  again saggin a Quebec 12 year that share  is less than ten percent and again  abbotsford Toronto and Windsor upwards  of sixteen to seventeen percent of of  individuals ranking their life  satisfaction as six or below now the  objective of this study was not the  document and explain the broad range or  to explain the variance across sea mas  but rather to highlight the fact that  these differences are there but  nonetheless what factors might explain  these differences that we could at least  start to address this so it may be the  case and I'll come to this in just a  minute it may be the case that there's  more unemployed folks in a certain CMA  or there's more immigrants in Vancouver  and Toronto than there are in the saggin  a or that household incomes might vary  across the sea mas and it might be that  the the cumulative effect of the  characteristics of the individuals in  these  gmas are indeed what account for the  differences that we're observing so what  we did is we ran some statistical  analysis and what this shows is how much  relative to the reference group specific  characteristic and I've just selected  some here how much specific  characteristics are associated with  differences in life satisfaction so  women are slightly more happy or more  satisfied with their life than men  immigrants slightly less compared to  individuals who are married the  coefficient for individuals who are  separated or divorced their minus point  6 on that 10-point scale or those who  are widowed or about a minus 4.6 on that  scale compared to individuals who are  employed the unemployed have a negative  employment has a negative effect of  about point 5 and similarly you see the  impact here for household differences  across household income relative to  those who have a household income of 100  to 150 thousand and again you see the  the strong correlation was self assess  health now if we take all of these  characteristics into account how much of  the difference across census  metropolitan areas remain well in fact  most of it about 85 to 90 percent so a  very small share is actually explained  by these individual level  characteristics the other thing that  might come to mind is Toronto and  Vancouver certainly are at the bottom of  that distribution so maybe it's strictly  an urban size effect that large cities  are that have something to do with  negative impact on life satisfaction so  what I'm showing in this last slide here  is we've done two things we've adjusted  for individual level socio economic  characteristics as I've mentioned and we  just did a simple categorization for  cmas that have less than 250 thousand  two hundred and fifty thousand to a  million and then large sea mas so even  there we see Toronto with life's this is  the percent scoring eight nine and ten  so about sixty-five percent of the  residents of Toronto after adjusting for  individual level characteristics score  an 89  10 compared to 71 or 72 percent in  Montreal if we look across small CMA  certainly I think there is through a  there's a size effect but I think what's  striking here is again controlling for  these characteristics places like Barry  and Guelph the percent you know around  65 66 percent of individual scoring in  eight nine or ten again with the  adjustment compared to over seventy five  percent of some of the Quebec sea mas so  quite clearly there's considerable  variation here so looking ahead I guess  my key point to be made here is that  information on life satisfaction is  increasingly deep and rich that our  cumulative sample of respondents that we  have in these surveys continues to grow  and that we could get both wrote more  robust estimates for the cmas we've  shown here and we also have scope to  disaggregate these census metropolitan  areas into smaller geographies with the  addition of the the sample I mentioned  coming up we'll have 20 22 24 thousand  responses for Vancouver alone so if we  want to look at Richmond you know Port  Coquitlam west van and so on we could  start to dig deeper and as we do that  across CMAs it gives us more more units  of analysis to look at it we can extend  our analysis the other thing that's  happening is the range of  characteristics or covariance that we  have for examining life satisfaction of  individuals continues to increase so we  hear that there's a lot that that the  importance of identity or social  belonging of participation of people's  ties with others is an important coral  or determinant of life satisfaction so  with the survey like the GSS on social  identity we can dig much deeper into  that and as we get recruit repeated as  we get cross-sectional files over time  that we do have increasing scope to  track life satisfaction over time among  specific groups for identifying broad  trends and there's been some work in  Canada suggesting or showing  a large increase in life satisfaction in  the province of Quebec relative to the  rest of Canada and as we as we have data  from two thousand onward we already got  a we have a span of 12 years it won't be  long before we left 20 years of data to  track those trends nonetheless I think  building on the previous studies to  identify the factors accounting for  inter cma differences in life  satisfaction is there still a tremendous  amount of work to be done but I think we  are fortunate in Canada that we have a  rich database and rich information to  bring statistical information to the  qualitative work that we see at the  ground level as well and it's the  combination of those two things that I  think is particularly powerful so thank  you very much thanks grant look forward  to hearing the questions from the  audience 44 in comments for the two of  you and for our next guest and by the  way in terms of interaction questions  comments exploration of some of the  things that you're doing all of that is  welcome and we can do it in a couple of  ways we're going to have mics move  around the audience or you can come up  front here and ask the question and have  a little bit of a dialogue with with our  panelists

